<div class="ephemera-page">

  <section class="ep-hero">
    <div class="ep-kicker">Physical organization · Storage reference</div>
    <h2>A place for everything</h2>
    <p>Protect the materials, keep them easy to browse, and make sure every item can be located using its inventory record.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Two primary storage locations</h2>
    <div class="ep-grid">
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>01 · Numbered binders</h3>
        <p>Most small-format materials are housed in three-ring binders using appropriate protective pocket sleeves.</p>
        <p>Binders provide a convenient way to browse individual pieces while keeping them organized and protected.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>02 · Flat file</h3>
        <p>Oversized materials and items unsuitable for standard binder pockets are stored in the designated flat-file space.</p>
        <p>Flat-file storage helps prevent folding, bending, and unnecessary handling.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Binder organization</h2>
    <p>Use the numbered binders and their pocket positions to establish a consistent physical location for each item.</p>

    <div class="ep-grid">
      <article class="ep-step">
        <div class="ep-number">01</div>
        <h3>Use protective sleeves</h3>
        <p>Place items in appropriate sleeves. Avoid forcing an item into a pocket that is too small or otherwise unsuitable.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-step">
        <div class="ep-number">02</div>
        <h3>Keep IDs visible</h3>
        <p>Write the ID on the sleeve or an appropriate archival label, not directly on the original item.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-step">
        <div class="ep-number">03</div>
        <h3>Record the pocket location</h3>
        <p>Use the binder number and page or pocket position to identify where the item is stored.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-step">
        <div class="ep-number">04</div>
        <h3>Update the inventory</h3>
        <p>Make sure the spreadsheet location matches the item's actual binder and pocket position.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="ep-example">
      <strong>Standard location format:</strong><br>
      <span class="ep-inline-code">Binder 2, p. 14</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Flat-file organization</h2>
    <p>Use the flat file for oversized materials that cannot be appropriately housed in standard binder sleeves.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Keep materials flat and appropriately protected.</li>
      <li>Avoid stacking items in ways that could cause bending or abrasion.</li>
      <li>Keep IDs associated with their individual protective housing.</li>
      <li>Record the actual drawer location in the inventory.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="ep-example">
      <strong>Standard location format:</strong><br>
      <span class="ep-inline-code">Flat File, Drawer 1</span>
    </div>
    <p>If a more detailed location is needed to retrieve an item, consult the librarian before introducing a new location convention.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Choosing appropriate housing</h2>
    <div class="ep-table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Material</th><th scope="col">General approach</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Small stickers</td><td>Appropriate protective pocket or archival housing.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Postcards</td><td>Protective pocket sized to prevent bending or slipping.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Flyers</td><td>Binder pocket when size and condition permit.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Minicomics</td><td>Protective pocket that accommodates the publication without forcing or compressing it.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Buttons/pins</td><td>Appropriate protective housing that prevents damage to the item or surrounding materials.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Posters and oversized prints</td><td>Flat-file storage when they cannot be safely accommodated in binders.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Other</td><td>Determine the appropriate housing based on size, material, and condition; ask the librarian when uncertain.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Handling and preservation</h2>
    <div class="ep-callout">
      <strong>Protect the original item.</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Never write directly on original materials.</li>
        <li>Do not apply adhesive labels to items.</li>
        <li>Handle fragile materials carefully.</li>
        <li>Do not fold or force materials into unsuitable pockets.</li>
        <li>Keep items in their assigned housing when not being processed or consulted.</li>
        <li>Refer damaged or questionable materials to the librarian.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Moving an item?</h2>
    <p>If an item is moved to another binder, pocket, or flat-file location, update its Location field in the inventory promptly.</p>
    <p>The ID stays with the item. A change in physical location does not require a new identifier.</p>
    <div class="ep-warning">
      <strong>Remember:</strong> Never reuse an ID or assign a new one simply because an item has been relocated.
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="ep-footer">Clark College Libraries · Comics &amp; Graphic Novels Ephemera · Housing reference</div>
</div>
